 ## Overview
-UV-based motion parameterization is a method for modeling dynamic human deformation by reparameterizing 4D space-time query points into a 3D surface-time domain using 2D UV coordinates and time [S1]. It solves the problem of high-dimensional motion field learning by exploiting the prior that human motion occurs primarily at the surface level, where points sharing similar UV coordinates exhibit similar deformation patterns [S1]. This approach significantly reduces the memory footprint and computational complexity of the residual deformation network compared to direct space-time parameterization [S1].
+UV-based motion parameterization is a technique for modeling dynamic human deformation by reparameterizing 3D spatial coordinates into 2D surface-level UV coordinates combined with time [S1]. It solves the problem of high-dimensional motion field learning by exploiting the prior that human motion occurs primarily at the surface level, reducing the input dimensionality from 4D space-time to 3D surface-time [S1]. This approach is significantly more memory efficient and converges faster than methods using raw 3D coordinates [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The method transforms a query point from world space to canonical space by combining Linear Blend Skinning (LBS) with a residual deformation network conditioned on UV coordinates and time [S1].
+The method refines the rigid transformation provided by Linear Blend Skinning (LBS) with a residual deformation network [S1]. It operates on query points in world space, transforming them to canonical space and then parameterizing them for the residual network [S1].
 
